{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_true-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-2_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, there are indeed wines that are considered sour, typically due to their high acidity. This sourness can come from naturally acidic grape varieties or from certain winemaking processes. For example, some white wines\u2014especially those made from grapes like Ugni Blanc, Folle Blanche, and Colombard\u2014are notably acidic. These grapes are actually used to make Cognac, precisely because their high acidity preserves aromas and flavors during distillation.\n\nSourness in wine is generally less desired for drinking, as excessively sour wine may taste unpleasant. Sometimes, if wine is exposed to bacteria, the alcohol can turn into acetic acid (vinegar), making it even more sour and unpalatable. However, controlled acidity in some wines, such as highly acclaimed Pinot Noirs, can add balance and freshness to the flavor profile without being overwhelmingly sour.\n\nGiven your taste preference for less sour flavors, you might want to avoid wines made from very high-acid grapes or wines that have undergone spoilage. If you\u2019re interested in exploring, you could compare a high-acid white wine with a smoother red to notice the difference.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0043-81-08016:6": 24.184906005859375, "clueweb22-en0043-98-11500:9": 23.222023010253906, "clueweb22-en0043-81-08016:4": 23.038772583007812, "clueweb22-en0022-23-12316:4": 23.030441284179688, "clueweb22-en0033-83-02323:11": 22.588640213012695, "clueweb22-en0022-23-12316:3": 22.39202880859375, "clueweb22-en0043-81-08016:5": 21.96879768371582, "clueweb22-en0009-15-09429:13": 21.681562423706055, "clueweb22-en0022-23-12316:2": 21.681440353393555, "clueweb22-en0012-09-15314:1": 21.652027130126953}, "ptkb_provenance": ["My grandfather used to have a winery in Great Britain."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0043-81-08016:6": 24.184906005859375, "clueweb22-en0043-98-1