{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_true-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-2_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Certainly! Wines can vary widely in their acidity, which contributes to their \"sourness.\" In the context of wine, \"sour\" typically refers to high acidity, resulting in a tart or sharp taste. Some white wines are known for their pronounced acidity, such as Sauvignon Blanc, Chenin Blanc, and certain white wines from the Loire Valley or Burgundy in France. Red wines can also be acidic, but this is more common in varieties like Pinot Noir or some Italian reds.\n\nOn the other hand, \"soft\" or \"supple\" wines are not overly tannic or acidic and tend to feel smooth on the palate. Examples include Merlot, which is approachable and low in acidity, or Beaujolais, which is light and soft.\n\nIf you are interested in exploring wines with different levels of acidity, you might start with approachable, fruit-forward reds like Zinfandel, Petite Sirah, or Merlot. For whites, consider Chardonnay or Pinot Grigio, which are often dry but not overly acidic.\n\nGiven your background and family connection to winemaking, you might also appreciate how climate and grape variety influence acidity and flavor. This could be a delightful way to connect with your grandfather\u2019s legacy while discovering your own preferences.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0040-18-06522:7": 24.297975540161133, "clueweb22-en0022-23-12316:4": 23.884552001953125, "clueweb22-en0012-09-15314:7": 23.118925094604492, "clueweb22-en0031-83-05665:8": 22.947769165039062, "clueweb22-en0011-37-14291:0": 22.818939208984375, "clueweb22-en0034-91-18132:10": 22.79874038696289, "clueweb22-en0010-50-16276:3": 22.536035537719727, "clueweb22-en0015-14-14349:18": 22.201448440551758, "clueweb22-en0019-13-00670:18": 22.18684196472168, "clueweb22-en0014-34-08039:34": 21.96371841430664}, "ptkb_provenance": ["My grandfather used to have a winery in Great Britain."]}], "references": {"c