{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_true-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Certainly. Finger tendon injuries, particularly to the flexor tendons, are common among climbers and often present as pain, limited finger movement, swelling, or, in some cases, a sensation of a \u201cpop.\u201d The flexor tendons run along the palm side of your fingers and are held close to the bone by structures called pulleys, with the A2 pulley being especially prone to injury from climbing.\n\nPulley injuries typically occur due to excessive force\u2014often from dynamic moves or crimping holds\u2014and can result in pain, swelling, and sometimes visible bowstringing of the tendon. It's important to distinguish between mild soreness and more serious injuries: if you notice marked swelling, persistent pain, or difficulty moving your finger, it\u2019s best to consult a doctor promptly, especially if you experience an open wound or significant functional loss.\n\nFor minor tendon pain, rest, avoiding strenuous use, and gradual rehabilitation are key. Many climbers find that pain subsides with time, but lingering discomfort and nervousness about re-injury are common. Prevention strategies include thorough warm-ups, using safer grip techniques, and gradually building finger strength with targeted exercises.\n\nIf you\u2019d like, I can explain how to assess your symptoms further or suggest some specific rehabilitation or prevention routines.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:11": 25.116256713867188, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 24.7304630279541, "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 24.49387550354004, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 24.406558990478516, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:7": 23.819276809692383, "clueweb22-en0013-93-15759:18": 23.79248809814453, "clueweb22-en0016-25-19413:13": 23.79248809814453, "clueweb22-en0038-00-07527:13": 23.747407913208008, "clueweb22-en0005-98-01404:21": 23.58098793029785, "clueweb22-en