{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_true-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, pain in the front of the finger during crimping\u2014especially without visible bowstringing\u2014can still indicate an A2 pulley injury. Pulley strains, particularly of the A2, are the most common finger injuries in climbers and are often caused by forceful use of the full crimp grip. Symptoms typically include localized pain and sometimes swelling at the base of the finger, even in the absence of obvious bowstringing.\n\nBowstringing is more likely when there is a complete rupture, while partial tears or strains may only cause pain and mild swelling. Since you do not see bowstringing, it is possible you have a lower-grade (Grade 1 or 2) injury rather than a complete rupture.\n\nIt is advisable to seek a diagnosis from a specialist to confirm the extent of the injury. Treatment for milder pulley injuries usually involves rest, gentle movement exercises, and avoiding aggravating activities like crimping until symptoms improve. If pain persists or worsens, further assessment and possible imaging might be needed.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 29.78801727294922, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 27.62247085571289, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 26.79227066040039, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": 26.712745666503906, "clueweb22-en0000-18-09663:3": 26.68475914001465, "clueweb22-en0000-18-09663:2": 25.60422134399414, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 25.46034049987793, "clueweb22-en0044-90-18231:0": 24.897052764892578, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 24.7466983795166, "clueweb22-en0000-18-09663:4": 24.355180740356445}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 29.78801727294922, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 27.62247085571289, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 26.79227066040039, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": 26.712745