{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_true-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Given your pain is at the front of the finger, especially when crimping, and without visible bowstringing, it is likely a strain or minor injury to the flexor tendon pulley (commonly the A2) rather than a complete rupture. Here are recommended next steps:\n\n1. **Rest and Activity Modification:** Avoid climbing or any movements that aggravate the pain, especially crimping, for at least several days. Allowing the tissue to recover is essential.\n\n2. **Gentle Exercises:** If your pain is mild and there is no loss of finger function, gentle range-of-motion exercises can be beneficial. Do not immobilize unless advised by a healthcare professional.\n\n3. **Taping:** If you return to climbing or daily activities, consider using H-taping, which is shown to be most effective in supporting injured pulleys and preventing bowstringing during recovery.\n\n4. **Monitor Symptoms:** Watch for increased swelling, persistent pain, or loss of function. If these occur, or if you are unsure about the severity, consult a doctor familiar with climbing injuries. They may use ultrasound or MRI for diagnosis.\n\n5. **Prevention:** Once healed, always warm up thoroughly, use proper technique, and avoid dynamic moves to small holds to reduce the risk of re-injury.\n\nIf you notice any worsening, see a specialist promptly.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 24.895572662353516, "clueweb22-en0000-18-09663:3": 24.064247131347656, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 23.16874885559082, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:18": 23.096952438354492, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:5": 22.866979598999023, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 22.822154998779297, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 22.781301498413086, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 22.49323844909668, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 22.4600772857666, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:2": 22.30026626586914}, "ptkb_prov