{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_true-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "That is a prudent decision. When you return to climbing after a finger pulley injury, it is important to follow a gradual and careful approach to prevent recurrence or worsening of the injury. Here are some key recommendations based on climbing-related finger injuries:\n\n1. **Progressive Loading**: Start with easy climbs and avoid any routes or problems that require crimping or high finger force, especially full crimp grips. Gradually increase intensity only if you are pain-free.\n\n2. **Warm-Up Thoroughly**: Always perform a comprehensive warm-up, including general aerobic activity and specific finger mobilization and stretching. This prepares the tendons and pulleys for loading.\n\n3. **Use Protective Taping**: Consider H-taping the affected finger, which can provide some support to the pulley and remind you to avoid excessive loading.\n\n4. **Monitor Symptoms**: If pain or swelling returns, stop climbing and allow more time for recovery. Persistent or worsening symptoms should prompt consultation with a climbing-knowledgeable physician.\n\n5. **Strengthening and Prevention**: Incorporate finger stretches and controlled exercises (such as mono hangs) into your regular training to build resilience and help prevent future injuries.\n\nBy following these steps, you will reduce your risk of re-injury and support your return to climbing in a safe and sustainable manner.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 31.454015731811523, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 29.53253746032715, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 28.530759811401367, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:8": 28.341215133666992, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 27.62986183166504, "clueweb22-en0000-18-09663:3": 27.5308837890625, "clueweb22-en0000-18-09663:2": 27.435392379760742, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 27.045969009399414, "clueweb22-en0034-09-06168:4": 25.579151