{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_true-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Taping is a common and effective way to protect your injured finger pulley as you return to climbing. For an A2 or A3 pulley injury, taping helps restrict excessive finger bending and prevents crimping, giving your pulley additional support.\n\nHere\u2019s a basic method recommended for pulley injuries:\n\n1. **Use 1.5\u20132.5 cm wide athletic tape.**\n2. **H-taping technique:**  \n   - Cut a strip of tape long enough to wrap around your finger twice.\n   - Tear the ends of the tape lengthwise, leaving the center intact (creating an \u201cH\u201d shape).\n   - Place the middle (un-torn) part over the palm side of your finger at the level of the injured pulley (usually just above the base of the finger).\n   - Wrap the split ends around the sides and back of your finger, overlapping slightly and pressing firmly but not so tight that circulation is restricted.\n\n3. **General tips:**  \n   - The tape should feel supportive, not constrictive.\n   - Reapply fresh tape before each session, especially if you sweat or if the tape loosens.\n   - Taping is most effective when combined with proper warm-up, gentle climbing, and attentive technique.\n\nRemember, taping supports recovery but is not a substitute for rest and gradual return. If pain persists, consider consulting a climbing-knowledgeable physician.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 32.069969177246094, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 31.954936981201172, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 30.45987892150879, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 29.14077377319336, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 28.672224044799805, "clueweb22-en0000-18-09663:3": 28.59278678894043, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": 28.401371002197266, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:1": 28.317049026489258, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:2": 28.152090072631836, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:8": 27.932945251464844}, "ptk