{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_true-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Thank you for clarifying. Meetings can indeed be a significant source of distraction, especially when you are experiencing hyperfocus or are deeply engaged in a task. People with ADHD often find it challenging to switch attention from a focused activity to an unrelated meeting, which can feel disruptive and make it difficult to regain focus afterward.\n\nHere are some strategies that might help:\n\n1. **Compartmentalize Meeting Times**: If possible, try to schedule meetings together in specific blocks of your day. This approach minimizes interruptions to your focused work and allows you to plan deep work sessions around meeting times.\n\n2. **Buffer Time**: Give yourself a short buffer before and after meetings. Use this time to make notes about where you left off, so you can return to your task more easily afterwards.\n\n3. **Prepare Mentally**: Before a meeting, jot down what you were working on and what your next step will be. This can help you quickly re-engage with your task post-meeting.\n\n4. **Communicate Needs**: If you find meetings particularly disruptive, consider discussing with your team or supervisor if some meetings could be reduced, condensed, or replaced with written updates.\n\nWould you like to go into more detail on any of these strategies?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0022-21-03294:11": 23.741159439086914, "clueweb22-en0016-28-02016:6": 23.32293701171875, "clueweb22-en0014-54-13628:2": 22.797731399536133, "clueweb22-en0044-44-00512:4": 22.707277297973633, "clueweb22-en0004-49-00618:3": 22.362871170043945, "clueweb22-en0032-61-13276:16": 22.174049377441406, "clueweb22-en0014-54-13628:1": 22.06741714477539, "clueweb22-en0017-98-03504:8": 22.05356788635254, "clueweb22-en0006-62-03234:3": 22.04989242553711, "clueweb22-en0034-01-04061:10": 21.90217399597168}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I love it when it is qu